{
  "rationale": "The merged front is 18/18 iterative and the smallest feasible area found anywhere is iterative at 261 LUTs+FFs; unrolled_k's best is 363 and it has never reached the front. The throughput constraint is trivially satisfied by both my families (iterative needs N+3 cycles/result, so even N=20 at a modest Fmax clears 1 MSPS by a wide margin), so the only live axis is area vs accuracy. I therefore put almost all of my budget into iterative: refine the small-area corner (where the selection rule min luts_plus_ffs will pick) and extend the accuracy end of the front out toward the HV reference area of 1500 so the hypervolume covers the whole curve. I keep a small unrolled_k share only to confirm there is no niche (e.g. very small N) where its extra barrel shifters pay off; structurally it should be dominated because it replicates the shifter k times while iterative shares one.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Owns the entire merged front and the global best area (261 LUTs+FFs). Accuracy depends only on W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, so I search the full guard/rounding space and sweep W from the feasibility boundary (~12, where output LSB 2^-(W-2) starts to clear 2^-10) up to 22 to push accuracy_bits toward the HV reference of 10 and area toward 1500. n_iter is capped at 20 because CORDIC residual error saturates near W bits, so larger N only burns cycles/area without buying accuracy.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 6, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "iterative",
      "budget_share": 0.85
    },
    {
      "why": "Kept only as a cheap confirmation. It meets the constraints but is structurally dominated by iterative for this spec: it instantiates k barrel shifters against iterative's single shared shifter for the same N, and its only advantage (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles) is irrelevant because throughput is not the binding constraint. I restrict it to small k (2..4) and small W/N where the area penalty is smallest, in case a very short schedule lets a tiny-N design slip under iterative on LUTs+FFs.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 6, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "budget_share": 0.15
    }
  ]
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "Round 1 mapped only the iterative family, and the feasible front is narrow relative to the registry: accuracy_bits spans 10.6..16.3 while the registry allows up to 28, and luts_plus_ffs spans 266..397 against an HV reference of 1500, so most of the dominated region is still empty. The low-area corner is already well pinned (best feasible luts_plus_ffs=266 at accuracy 10.6, just above the 2^-10 constraint), and the constraint throughput_msps>=1 is met with huge margin (best 22 MSPS), so the remaining HV gain is in extending the front toward higher accuracy and confirming the area floor. With 100 of 200 evals left, spending this round on NSGA-II over the full ranges of the family already on the front (iterative, seeded with the 12-design front) is the best use of budget before any family expansion; unrolled_k showed only 2 feasible designs and no better area, and pipelined/pipelined_m are unlikely to help a min-area objective.",
  "next_plan": null
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round and the 200-eval budget is fully spent (200/200, round 3 of 4), so no further refinement, widening, adding or full-front mapping can be paid for. The spec is met with margin: throughput constraint has 0% violations (best 22 MSPS vs >=1 MSPS required) and accuracy has feasible designs down to max_abs_err 7.8e-08 (2^-23.61), far below the 2^-10 = 9.77e-04 limit. The feasible Pareto front is broad and well-covered (27 designs, luts_plus_ffs 266..688 against HV ref 1500, accuracy_bits 10.6..23.6 against HV ref 10), hypervolume 1.432e+04 with a +15.4% gain this round, so the trade-off curve is mapped reasonably for the budget. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) picks iterative [data_width=16 n_iter=16 angle_guard=0 frac_guard=0 rounding=trunc] at 266 LUTs+FFs with accuracy_bits=10.6 (max_abs_err 6.43e-04 <= 9.77e-04) and 10.4 MSPS, which is feasible. The family space is exhausted too: iterative and unrolled_k both explored, no unexplored family left. No constraint is unreachable, so infeasible is not justified; stop is."
}
